<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training
<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span> <sup id="id7" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗</span></sup> A. Morgan and U. Khan contributed equally to this work. </span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alexander Morgan<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗</span></sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dept. of EECS, MIT
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Cambridge, MA, USA 
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ummay Sumaya Khan<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗</span></sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dept. of ECE, Virginia Tech
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Alexandria, VA, USA 
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Lingjia Liu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dept. of ECE, Virginia Tech
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Alexandria, VA, USA 
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Lizhong Zheng
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dept. of EECS, MIT
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Cambridge, MA, USA  
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recurrent neural networks (RNNs) can be interpreted as discrete-time state-space models, where the state evolution corresponds to an infinite-impulse-response (IIR) filtering operation governed by both feedforward weights and recurrent poles. While, in principle, all parameters including pole locations can be optimized via backpropagation through time (BPTT), such joint learning incurs substantial computational overhead and is often impractical for applications with limited training data.
Echo state networks (ESNs) mitigate this limitation by fixing the recurrent dynamics and training only a linear readout, enabling efficient and stable online adaptation. In this work, we analytically and empirically examine why learning recurrent poles does not provide tangible benefits in data-constrained, real-time learning scenarios. Our analysis shows that pole learning renders the weight optimization problem highly non-convex, requiring significantly more training samples and iterations for gradient-based methods to converge to meaningful solutions. Empirically, we observe that for complex-valued data, gradient descent frequently exhibits prolonged plateaus, and advanced optimizers offer limited improvement. In contrast, fixed-pole architectures induce stable and well-conditioned state representations even with limited training data. Numerical results demonstrate that fixed-pole networks achieve superior performance with lower training complexity, making them more suitable for online real-time tasks.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Recurrent neural network (RNN), state space model (SSM), gradient descent (GD), echo state network (ESN), reservoir computing (RC), non-convex optimization.

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recurrent neural networks (RNNs) are a popular method for modeling sequential data with applications ranging from communications system design to audio and natural language processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Despite their representational power, traditional RNNs require extensive backpropagation through time (BPTT) for weight updates, which imposes significant computational burden and demands large volumes of labeled data, often unattainable in low-latency wireless settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Echo State Networks (ESNs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> have emerged as a lightweight alternative to conventional RNNs by eliminating the need to train recurrent connections. In this framework, the internal reservoir dynamics are governed by fixed, randomly initialized weights, and only the output layer undergoes supervised adaptation. This architectural decoupling substantially reduces computational burden, making ESNs well-suited for low-latency training in scenarios where labeled data and pilot resources are limited such as wireless signal detection and channel equalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent developments have moved beyond random reservoir design by leveraging structural information from the physical domain to inform weight initialization. In particular, the studies in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> provide a formal analysis of reservoir computing applied to wireless channel equalization. These works show that when the recurrent weights are configured to mirror the characteristics of minimum-phase (MP) channels, it can achieve near-optimal bit-error rate (BER) performance, aligning with fundamental theoretical limits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, no prior work provides a theoretical and empirical investigation into the fundamental limitations of learning recurrent dynamics in RNNs for low-latency signal processing tasks. In this work, we show that gradient descent (GD) based recurrent pole learning induces a highly non-convex and ill-conditioned optimization problem, and lack of convexity and unbounded condition numbers pose key challenges leading to optimization plateaus, even in linear settings. Experiments on wireless symbol detection validate the analysis, demonstrating that fixed-pole reservoir architectures exhibit stable training and competitive performance compared to trainable RNNs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This paper is structured as follows: Section <a href="#S2" title="II RNN pole learning via BPTT ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> reviews the fundamentals of RNN training via BPTT. Section <a href="#S3" title="III Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> analyzes the limitations of gradient-based pole learning in RNNs. Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Empirical Results ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> presents empirical validation. Section <a href="#S5" title="V Conclusion ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> concludes the work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RNN pole learning via BPTT</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a linear single-input and single-output RNN with a single recurrent neuron and identity activation. Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x[t]\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x[t]\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input signal and <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h[t]\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h[t]\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the hidden state of the neuron at time <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The linear state update equation is:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h[t]=w_{\mathrm{rec}}\,h[t-1]+w_{\mathrm{in}}\,x[t]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h[t]=w_{\mathrm{rec}}\,h[t-1]+w_{\mathrm{in}}\,x[t],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\mathrm{rec}}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\mathrm{rec}}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the recurrent feedback weight and <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\mathrm{in}}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\mathrm{in}}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input coupling weight. Taking the <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-transform under zero initial conditions yields</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H(z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=w_{\mathrm{rec}}z^{-1}H(z)+w_{\mathrm{in}}X(z)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=w_{\mathrm{rec}}z^{-1}H(z)+w_{\mathrm{in}}X(z),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{H(z)}{X(z)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{H(z)}{X(z)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{w_{\mathrm{in}}}{1-w_{\mathrm{rec}}z^{-1}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{w_{\mathrm{in}}}{1-w_{\mathrm{rec}}z^{-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, each recurrent neuron realizes a first-order IIR filter with pole located at <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\mathrm{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\mathrm{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> and feedforward gain <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\mathrm{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\mathrm{in}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now extend this interpretation to a network of <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> recurrent neurons. Let <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}[t]\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\mathrm{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}[t]\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\mathrm{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input vector at time <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}[t]\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}[t]\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the hidden state vector. The linear RNN dynamics become <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}[t]=\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}\,\mathbf{h}[t-1]+\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{in}}\,\mathbf{x}[t]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}[t]=\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}\,\mathbf{h}[t-1]+\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{in}}\,\mathbf{x}[t],</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{h}\times N_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{h}\times N_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the recurrent weight matrix and <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{in}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{h}\times N_{\mathrm{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{in}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{h}\times N_{\mathrm{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input weight matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The output at time <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{y}}[t]=\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{out}}\,\mathbf{h}[t]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{y}}[t]=\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{out}}\,\mathbf{h}[t],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">with output weight matrix, <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{out}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\mathrm{out}}\times N_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{out}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\mathrm{out}}\times N_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Given a target sequence <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\mathrm{out}}\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\mathrm{out}}\times T}</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequence-level loss is defined as <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\hat{\mathbf{Y}},\mathbf{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\hat{\mathbf{Y}},\mathbf{Y})</annotation></semantics></math>. In conventional RNN training, all parameters <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}\in\{\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{in}},\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}},\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{out}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}\in\{\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{in}},\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}},\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{out}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are optimized jointly using BPTT to learn the system dynamics. The gradient with respect to any weight <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\mathbf{W}}=\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\mathbf{h}[t]}\frac{\partial\mathbf{h}[t]}{\partial\mathbf{W}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝐖</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝐖</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\mathbf{W}}=\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\mathbf{h}[t]}\frac{\partial\mathbf{h}[t]}{\partial\mathbf{W}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The backpropagated state gradients obey the recursion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\mathbf{h}[t]}=\sum_{k=t}^{T-1}(\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}^{\top})^{k-t}\left(\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{out}}^{\top}\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\hat{\mathbf{y}}[k]}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>rec</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>out</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\mathbf{h}[t]}=\sum_{k=t}^{T-1}(\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}^{\top})^{k-t}\left(\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{out}}^{\top}\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\hat{\mathbf{y}}[k]}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">which reveals the dependence of gradient flow on the spectral structure of <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in repeated propagation of error gradients, amplifying or attenuating based on pole magnitudes. Under linearization, <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> is diagonalizable with eigenvalues <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\lambda_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\lambda_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the recurrent dynamics can be interpreted as a bank of parallel, non-interacting IIR filters with poles <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Consequently, the gradient with respect to each pole scales as</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\lambda_{i}}\propto\sum_{k=t}^{T-1}\lambda_{i}^{k-t},\vskip-1.4457pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">∝</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\lambda_{i}}\propto\sum_{k=t}^{T-1}\lambda_{i}^{k-t},\vskip-1.4457pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">which grows or decays exponentially depending on <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda_{i}|&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda_{i}|&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, gradients explode; when <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda_{i}|&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda_{i}|&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, gradients vanish.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, ESNs circumvent this difficulty by fixing the recurrent dynamics. The recurrent matrix <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> is randomly initialized and its spectral radius satisfies <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}})&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}})&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring stability. The matrices <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> are kept fixed, which effectively fixes the associated pole locations. Learning is restricted to the output layer, where the weights are obtained by solving a convex least‑squares optimization problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{out}}=\argmin_{\mathbf{W}}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\|\mathbf{y}[t]-\mathbf{W}_{out}\;\mathbf{h}[t]\|_{2}^{2}=\mathbf{YH}^{\dagger}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>𝐖</mi></msub><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.0835em">=</mo><msup><mi>𝐘𝐇</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{out}}=\argmin_{\mathbf{W}}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\|\mathbf{y}[t]-\mathbf{W}_{out}\;\mathbf{h}[t]\|_{2}^{2}=\mathbf{YH}^{\dagger},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">where, <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\cdot)^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\cdot)^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Moore–Penrose pseudo-inverse and <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{h}\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{h}\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the state matrix. This decoupling of recurrent dynamics from training avoids pole optimization entirely, enabling stable and efficient optimization while preserving temporal dynamics with fixed poles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we analytically show that in a noiseless setting where pole learning is identifiable, even though standard optimization formulations for learning pole locations should work in principle, they are inherently ill-conditioned and non-convex, so they often fail in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\{(b_{k},\beta_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\{(b_{k},\beta_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k},\beta_{k}\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k},\beta_{k}\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, be a set of parameters describing a causal impulse response of the form</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h[n;B]=\begin{cases}\sum_{k=1}^{K}b_{k}\beta_{k}^{n}&amp;\text{ if }n\geq 0\\
0&amp;\text{ if }n&lt;0\end{cases}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h[n;B]=\begin{cases}\sum_{k=1}^{K}b_{k}\beta_{k}^{n}&amp;\text{ if }n\geq 0\\
0&amp;\text{ if }n&lt;0\end{cases}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Note that we consider <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> to be unordered with respect to <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Unless otherwise stated, whenever we work with such a set of parameters, we will assume them in fully simplified form.
In particular, we assume that poles are pairwise distinct, i.e.,
for <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\neq k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\neq k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{k}\neq\beta_{k^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{k}\neq\beta_{k^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and that coefficients are non-zero, i.e., <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Achieving this form is always possible by combining terms with the same pole and dropping terms with zero coefficient.
Given a collection of input-output pairs <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{(r)}[\cdot],y^{(r)}[\cdot])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{(r)}[\cdot],y^{(r)}[\cdot])</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{(r)}[n]=(h[\cdot;B]*x^{(r)})[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(r)}[n]=(h[\cdot;B]*x^{(r)})[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, our objective is to recover the parameters <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our first goal is to characterize when this is possible to better understand the problem of learning pole locations <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> from observed datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma III.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise distinct but otherwise unrestricted <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1},\dots,\beta_{K}\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1},\dots,\beta_{K}\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and choose any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq K</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}[n]</annotation></semantics></math> be the length-<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sequence defined by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}[n]=\beta_{k}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}[n]=\beta_{k}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent, i.e., if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1},\dots,b_{K}\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1},\dots,b_{K}\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=1}^{K}b_{k}s_{k}[n]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=1}^{K}b_{k}s_{k}[n]=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume without loss of generality that <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=K</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;\cdots&amp;1\\
\beta_{1}&amp;\beta_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;\beta_{K}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
\beta_{1}^{K-1}&amp;\beta_{2}^{K-1}&amp;\cdots&amp;\beta_{K}^{K-1}\end{bmatrix}}_{\triangleq V^{\top}(\beta_{1},\dots,\beta_{K})}\begin{bmatrix}b_{1}\\
b_{2}\\
\vdots\\
b_{K}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}0\\
0\\
\vdots\\
0\end{bmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≜</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;\cdots&amp;1\\
\beta_{1}&amp;\beta_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;\beta_{K}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
\beta_{1}^{K-1}&amp;\beta_{2}^{K-1}&amp;\cdots&amp;\beta_{K}^{K-1}\end{bmatrix}}_{\triangleq V^{\top}(\beta_{1},\dots,\beta_{K})}\begin{bmatrix}b_{1}\\
b_{2}\\
\vdots\\
b_{K}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}0\\
0\\
\vdots\\
0\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a Vandermonde matrix with</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(V^{\top}(\beta_{1},\dots,\beta_{K}))=\prod_{1\leq k&lt;k^{\prime}\leq K}(\beta_{k^{\prime}}-\beta_{k})\neq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(V^{\top}(\beta_{1},\dots,\beta_{K}))=\prod_{1\leq k&lt;k^{\prime}\leq K}(\beta_{k^{\prime}}-\beta_{k})\neq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">we conclude that <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=b_{1}=\dots=b_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=b_{1}=\dots=b_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we show that if the impulse responses for two parameter sets <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B,\tilde{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B,\tilde{B}</annotation></semantics></math> match for sufficiently many samples, then it implies <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\tilde{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\tilde{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, given sufficiently many observations, it is in principle possible to determine the parameters describing the impulse response.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem III.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and fix a set of parameters <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\{(b_{k},\beta_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\{(b_{k},\beta_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k},\beta_{k}\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k},\beta_{k}\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose we observe the first <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 2K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 2K</annotation></semantics></math> samples <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0,\dots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0,\dots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math> from the causal impulse response <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h[n;B]=\sum_{k=1}^{K}b_{k}\beta_{k}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h[n;B]=\sum_{k=1}^{K}b_{k}\beta_{k}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, it is possible to recover the values <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(b_{k},\beta_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(b_{k},\beta_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Formally, for any other alternative choice of parameters <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{B}=\{(\tilde{b}_{k},\tilde{\beta}_{k})\}_{k=1}^{\tilde{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{B}=\{(\tilde{b}_{k},\tilde{\beta}_{k})\}_{k=1}^{\tilde{K}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}\leq K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}\leq K</annotation></semantics></math>, if</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h[n;B]=h[n;\tilde{B}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h[n;B]=h[n;\tilde{B}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\tilde{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\tilde{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}=K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}=K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{B}^{\text{neg}}\triangleq\{(-\tilde{b}_{k},\tilde{\beta}_{k})\}_{k=1}^{\tilde{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>neg</mtext></msup><mo>≜</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{B}^{\text{neg}}\triangleq\{(-\tilde{b}_{k},\tilde{\beta}_{k})\}_{k=1}^{\tilde{K}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider a combined set of parameters <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(g_{m},\gamma_{m})\}_{m=1}^{M}=B\cup\tilde{B}^{\text{neg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>neg</mtext></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(g_{m},\gamma_{m})\}_{m=1}^{M}=B\cup\tilde{B}^{\text{neg}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\leq N</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\gamma_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\gamma_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct, i.e., we combine terms with the same <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, coefficients <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> may equal zero, so we temporarily disregard the full simplification assumption from earlier with respect to <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(g_{m},\gamma_{m})\}_{m=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(g_{m},\gamma_{m})\}_{m=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta[n]\triangleq h[n;B]-h[n;\tilde{B}]=\sum_{m=1}^{M}g_{m}\gamma_{m}^{n}=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta[n]\triangleq h[n;B]-h[n;\tilde{B}]=\sum_{m=1}^{M}g_{m}\gamma_{m}^{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">for all <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma III.1. ‣ III Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.1</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0=g_{m}=\left(\sum_{k:\beta_{k}=\gamma_{m}}b_{k}\right)-\left(\sum_{k:\tilde{\beta}_{k}=\gamma_{m}}\tilde{b}_{k}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=g_{m}=\left(\sum_{k:\beta_{k}=\gamma_{m}}b_{k}\right)-\left(\sum_{k:\tilde{\beta}_{k}=\gamma_{m}}\tilde{b}_{k}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">for all <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. These sums both contain exactly one term, which must also be nonzero. It follows that <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=M=\tilde{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=M=\tilde{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p5.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0\neq\sum_{k:\beta_{k}=\gamma_{m}}b_{k}=\sum_{k:\tilde{\beta}_{k}=\gamma_{m}}\tilde{b}_{k}\triangleq w_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.111em">≠</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≜</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\neq\sum_{k:\beta_{k}=\gamma_{m}}b_{k}=\sum_{k:\tilde{\beta}_{k}=\gamma_{m}}\tilde{b}_{k}\triangleq w_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{m},\gamma_{m})\in B\cap\tilde{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{m},\gamma_{m})\in B\cap\tilde{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, <math id="S3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\tilde{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\tilde{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note that if <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N&lt;2K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N&lt;2K</annotation></semantics></math>, then impulse response representations may not be unique. For instance, consider any pairwise distinct parameters <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\beta_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}\cup\{\tilde{\beta}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}\cup\{\tilde{\beta}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> , i.e., <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\{\beta_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}\cup\{\tilde{\beta}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}|=2K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\{\beta_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}\cup\{\tilde{\beta}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}|=2K</annotation></semantics></math>. By dimensionality constraints, there must exist <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k},\tilde{b}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k},\tilde{b}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> not all equal to <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> (by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma III.1. ‣ III Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.1</span></a>, all <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k},\tilde{b}_{k}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k},\tilde{b}_{k}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2K-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2K-1</annotation></semantics></math>) such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta[n]=h[n;B]-h[n;\tilde{B}]=\sum_{k=1}^{K}b_{k}\beta_{k}^{n}-\sum_{k=1}^{K}\tilde{b}_{k}\tilde{\beta}_{k}^{n}\\
=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta[n]=h[n;B]-h[n;\tilde{B}]=\sum_{k=1}^{K}b_{k}\beta_{k}^{n}-\sum_{k=1}^{K}\tilde{b}_{k}\tilde{\beta}_{k}^{n}\\
=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">for all <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, when <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is not sufficiently large, it is possible that <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\neq\tilde{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>≠</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\neq\tilde{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Given at least <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2K</annotation></semantics></math> samples of the impulse response, it is theoretically possible to determine the system parameters, as Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem III.2. ‣ III Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.2</span></a> shows. All that is required is a thorough search over all possible system parameters, checking to see if the first <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2K</annotation></semantics></math> samples of the candidate impulse response matches the desired response. But it is in fact possible to determine the system parameters given at least <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2K</annotation></semantics></math> samples of any (reasonable) input-output example pair.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma III.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose a causal signal <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x[n]</annotation></semantics></math> is filtered using a causal impulse response <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h[n]</annotation></semantics></math> to produce <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, and we observe the first <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> samples of this input-output pair. In particular, we have knowledge of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x[0],\dots,x[N-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x[0],\dots,x[N-1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y[0],\dots,y[N-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y[0],\dots,y[N-1]</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x[0]\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x[0]\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then it is possible to recover <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h[n]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is another causal impulse response with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y[n]=(\tilde{h}*x)[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y[n]=(\tilde{h}*x)[n]</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}[n]=h[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}[n]=h[n]</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Write</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}x_{0}&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;0\\
x_{1}&amp;x_{0}&amp;\cdots&amp;0\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
x_{N-1}&amp;x_{N-2}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{0}\end{bmatrix}}_{\triangleq L}\begin{bmatrix}h_{0}\\
h_{1}\\
\vdots\\
h_{N-1}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}y_{0}\\
y_{1}\\
\vdots\\
y_{N-1}\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≜</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}x_{0}&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;0\\
x_{1}&amp;x_{0}&amp;\cdots&amp;0\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
x_{N-1}&amp;x_{N-2}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{0}\end{bmatrix}}_{\triangleq L}\begin{bmatrix}h_{0}\\
h_{1}\\
\vdots\\
h_{N-1}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}y_{0}\\
y_{1}\\
\vdots\\
y_{N-1}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">with <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\triangleq x[0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\triangleq x[0]</annotation></semantics></math> and similarly for <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. The lower-triangular matrix <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(L)=(x[0])^{n}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(L)=(x[0])^{n}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, so we can invert <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> to find <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h[0],\dots,h[N-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h[0],\dots,h[N-1]</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x[0],\dots,x[N-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x[0],\dots,x[N-1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y[0],\dots,y[N-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y[0],\dots,y[N-1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Combining Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem III.2. ‣ III Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.2</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma III.3. ‣ III Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.3</span></a> yields</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary III.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and fix a set of parameters <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\{(b_{k},\beta_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\{(b_{k},\beta_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k},\beta_{k}\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k},\beta_{k}\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose we observe the first <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 2K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 2K</annotation></semantics></math> samples <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0,\dots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0,\dots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math> of any input-output pair <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x[\cdot],y[\cdot])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x[\cdot],y[\cdot])</annotation></semantics></math> related by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y[n]=(h[\cdot;B]*x)[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y[n]=(h[\cdot;B]*x)[n]</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x[0]\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x[0]\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then it is possible to recover <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally, the only choice of parameters <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{B}=\{(\tilde{b}_{k},\tilde{\beta}_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{B}=\{(\tilde{b}_{k},\tilde{\beta}_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y[n]=(h[\cdot;\tilde{B}]*x)[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y[n]=(h[\cdot;\tilde{B}]*x)[n]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{B}=B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{B}=B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma III.3. ‣ III Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.3</span></a>, <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y[n]=(h[\cdot;B]*x)[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y[n]=(h[\cdot;B]*x)[n]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y[n]=(h[\cdot;\tilde{B}]*x)[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y[n]=(h[\cdot;\tilde{B}]*x)[n]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math> together imply that <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h[n;B]=h[n;\tilde{B}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h[n;B]=h[n;\tilde{B}]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem III.2. ‣ III Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.2</span></a>, <math id="S3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\tilde{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\tilde{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The prior results demonstrate that learning system parameters is in principle possible under very mild assumptions. In particular, provided sufficiently many samples of any non-zero input-output pair, we can recover the system parameters by searching over all possible configurations and selecting the unique system that matches the observed data. However, this approach is not computationally feasible, especially in high dimensions, i.e., when <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> becomes large.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">These results will also show, in fact, that standard optimization objectives designed to recover these parameters will generally be non-convex, so in practice, recovery of system parameters is not straightforward. One trait that prevents convexity is lack of ordering among parameters, and pole locations in particular. Consider an unordered set of parameters <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\{(b_{k},\beta_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\{(b_{k},\beta_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and an associated ordered set <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\text{ord}}=((b_{k},\beta_{k}))_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\text{ord}}=((b_{k},\beta_{k}))_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be viewed as a vector in <math id="S3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Standard optimization problems over ordered parameters in <math id="S3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2K}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is required in practical algorithms such as gradient descent, leads to non-convex objectives. In particular,</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma III.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 2K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 2K</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\{(b_{k},\beta_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\{(b_{k},\beta_{k})\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of unordered parameters, let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\text{ord}}=((b_{k},\beta_{k}))_{k=1}^{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{2K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\text{ord}}=((b_{k},\beta_{k}))_{k=1}^{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{2K}</annotation></semantics></math> be any associated ordered set, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> be any non-identity permutation on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(B^{\text{ord}})\triangleq\sigma\left(((b_{k},\beta_{k}))_{k=1}^{K}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{2K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(B^{\text{ord}})\triangleq\sigma\left(((b_{k},\beta_{k}))_{k=1}^{K}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{2K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda B^{\text{ord}}+(1-\lambda)\sigma(B^{\text{ord}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>ord</mtext></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda B^{\text{ord}}+(1-\lambda)\sigma(B^{\text{ord}})</annotation></semantics></math> as an unordered set of parameters, does not equal <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, with slight abuse of notation, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h[\cdot;\lambda B^{\text{ord}}+(1-\lambda)\sigma(B^{\text{ord}})]\neq h[\cdot;B]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>ord</mtext></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h[\cdot;\lambda B^{\text{ord}}+(1-\lambda)\sigma(B^{\text{ord}})]\neq h[\cdot;B]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">The poles cannot remain in the same locations for <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|\lambda\beta_{k}+(1-\lambda)\beta_{\sigma(k)}|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|\lambda\beta_{k}+(1-\lambda)\beta_{\sigma(k)}|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{k}\lambda|\beta_{k}|^{2}+(1-\lambda)|\beta_{\sigma(k)}|^{2}-\lambda(1-\lambda)|\beta_{k}-\beta_{\sigma(k)}|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{k}\lambda|\beta_{k}|^{2}+(1-\lambda)|\beta_{\sigma(k)}|^{2}-\lambda(1-\lambda)|\beta_{k}-\beta_{\sigma(k)}|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{k}|\beta_{k}|^{2}-\lambda(1-\lambda)|\beta_{k}-\beta_{\sigma(k)}|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{k}|\beta_{k}|^{2}-\lambda(1-\lambda)|\beta_{k}-\beta_{\sigma(k)}|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle&lt;\sum_{k}|\beta_{k}|^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle&lt;\sum_{k}|\beta_{k}|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">In fact, this proof can be used to show that the same result holds for other parameterizations. For example, if <math id="S3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are expressed in polar coordinates, then we can still apply the prior analysis to the (real-valued) polar coordinates directly.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Under the assumptions and with the setup of Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary III.4. ‣ III Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.4</span></a> (importantly, we assume <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 2K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 2K</annotation></semantics></math>), a typical objective used to optimize over ordered estimated parameters <math id="S3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}};x[\cdot],y[\cdot])=d(y[\cdot],(h[\cdot;\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}}]*x)[\cdot])" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>;</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}};x[\cdot],y[\cdot])=d(y[\cdot],(h[\cdot;\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}}]*x)[\cdot])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p14.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\cdot,\cdot)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\cdot,\cdot)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is a measure of distance between two length-<math id="S3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> vectors with the property that <math id="S3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(a,b)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(a,b)=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=b</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g., <math id="S3.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(a,b)=\|a-b\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(a,b)=\|a-b\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also allow <math id="S3.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for the purpose of analysis. Let <math id="S3.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\text{ord}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\text{ord}}</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary ordering on <math id="S3.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary non-identity permutation on <math id="S3.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma III.5. ‣ III Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.5</span></a>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(B^{\text{ord}};x[\cdot],y[\cdot])=\mathcal{L}(\sigma(B^{\text{ord}});x[\cdot],y[\cdot])=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(B^{\text{ord}};x[\cdot],y[\cdot])=\mathcal{L}(\sigma(B^{\text{ord}});x[\cdot],y[\cdot])=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p14.3" class="ltx_p">but for any <math id="S3.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\lambda B^{\text{ord}}+(1-\lambda)\sigma(B^{\text{ord}});x[\cdot],y[\cdot])&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>ord</mtext></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\lambda B^{\text{ord}}+(1-\lambda)\sigma(B^{\text{ord}});x[\cdot],y[\cdot])&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">For any objective with the property that <math id="S3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{B}=B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{B}=B</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> being the ground-truth system, the same result holds, i.e., we lose convexity. Even though the system parameters may be in principle recoverable, it can be difficult in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming poles are now inside the unit circle, let</p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(e^{i\omega};B)=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\frac{b_{k}}{1-\beta_{k}e^{-i\omega}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mfrac><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(e^{i\omega};B)=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\frac{b_{k}}{1-\beta_{k}e^{-i\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p16.2" class="ltx_p">be the DTFT of <math id="S3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h[\cdot;B]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h[\cdot;B]</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider the objective</p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}};B)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}|H(e^{i\omega};\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}})-H(e^{i\omega};B)|^{2}\,d\omega" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}};B)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}|H(e^{i\omega};\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}})-H(e^{i\omega};B)|^{2}\,d\omega</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p16.3" class="ltx_p">which has <math id="S3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}};B)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}};B)=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(e^{i\omega};\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}})=H(e^{i\omega};B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(e^{i\omega};\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}})=H(e^{i\omega};B)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, since the full impulse response can be recovered by an inverse transform, <math id="S3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}};B)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}};B)=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{B}=B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{B}=B</annotation></semantics></math>, so convexity over <math id="S3.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}}</annotation></semantics></math> does not hold. The same applies to the objective</p>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime\prime}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}};B)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\left|1-\frac{H(e^{i\omega};\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}})}{H(e^{i\omega};B)}\right|^{2}\,d\omega," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime\prime}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}};B)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\left|1-\frac{H(e^{i\omega};\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}})}{H(e^{i\omega};B)}\right|^{2}\,d\omega,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p16.4" class="ltx_p">which is a more direct consideration in channel equalization, and more generally, for any <math id="S3.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\omega)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\omega)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> to</p>
<table id="S3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime\prime\prime}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}};B)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}w(\omega)|H(e^{i\omega};\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}})-H(e^{i\omega};B)|^{2}\,d\omega." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime\prime\prime}(\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}};B)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}w(\omega)|H(e^{i\omega};\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}})-H(e^{i\omega};B)|^{2}\,d\omega.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we show that the objective <math id="S3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> can have Hessian with unbounded condition number at an optimal point <math id="S3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}}=B^{\text{ord}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>ord</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>ord</mtext></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{B}^{\text{ord}}=B^{\text{ord}}</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to “narrow-valley” behavior. For a symmetric square matrix <math id="S3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(A)=\lambda^{\text{max}}/\lambda^{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(A)=\lambda^{\text{max}}/\lambda^{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the condition number of <math id="S3.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. We will first consider a simple example where the condition number becomes unbounded. Then, we can extend this to the more general situation described above.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21454v1/narrow_valley.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:238/252;" width="238" height="252" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>Surface of <math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=0.75</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=0.8</annotation></semantics></math>. Green and purple points represent <math id="S3.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,d)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,c)</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Note the “narrow-valley” behavior of the objective at the optimum points, reflective of a large condition number for the Hessian.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21454v1/rnn_training_dynamics_with_esn_baselines.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/133;" width="476" height="133" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Training dynamics of a trainable RNN. Left: gradient norm decay. Middle: condition number of recurrent matrix <math id="S3.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathrm{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathrm{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Right: training loss.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21454v1/spectral_radius_density.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/235;" width="381" height="235" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Spectral density of eigenvalue magnitudes <math id="S3.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math> over epochs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Fix two real numbers <math id="S3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,d\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,d\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=2</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose the ground-truth system is described by <math id="S3.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\{(1,c),(1,d)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\{(1,c),(1,d)\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose we wish to learn the locations of the poles by minimizing the objective</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}|h[n;B]-h[n;\tilde{B}]|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}|h[n;B]-h[n;\tilde{B}]|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\left(x^{n}+y^{n}-c^{n}-d^{n}\right)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\left(x^{n}+y^{n}-c^{n}-d^{n}\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p18.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{B}=\{(1,x),(1,y)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{B}=\{(1,x),(1,y)\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in(-1,1)\subset\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in(-1,1)\subset\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. The hessian of <math id="S3.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> evaluated at an optimal point <math id="S3.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)=(c,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)=(c,d)</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{f}(c,d)=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{2\left(1+c^{2}\right)}{\left(1-c^{2}\right)^{3}}&amp;\frac{2\left(1+cd\right)}{\left(1-cd\right)^{3}}\\
\frac{2\left(1+cd\right)}{\left(1-cd\right)^{3}}&amp;\frac{2\left(1+d^{2}\right)}{\left(1-d^{2}\right)^{3}}\end{bmatrix}=2\begin{bmatrix}\langle u_{c},u_{c}\rangle&amp;\langle u_{c},u_{d}\rangle\\
\langle u_{d},u_{c}\rangle&amp;\langle u_{d},u_{d}\rangle\end{bmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{f}(c,d)=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{2\left(1+c^{2}\right)}{\left(1-c^{2}\right)^{3}}&amp;\frac{2\left(1+cd\right)}{\left(1-cd\right)^{3}}\\
\frac{2\left(1+cd\right)}{\left(1-cd\right)^{3}}&amp;\frac{2\left(1+d^{2}\right)}{\left(1-d^{2}\right)^{3}}\end{bmatrix}=2\begin{bmatrix}\langle u_{c},u_{c}\rangle&amp;\langle u_{c},u_{d}\rangle\\
\langle u_{d},u_{c}\rangle&amp;\langle u_{d},u_{d}\rangle\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p18.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\alpha|&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\alpha|&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> is the square-summable sequence <math id="S3.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\alpha}[n]=(n+1)\alpha^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\alpha}[n]=(n+1)\alpha^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D=\det\left(\frac{1}{2}H_{f}(c,d)\right)=\langle u_{c},u_{c}\rangle\langle u_{d},u_{d}\rangle-\langle u_{c},u_{d}\rangle^{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D=\det\left(\frac{1}{2}H_{f}(c,d)\right)=\langle u_{c},u_{c}\rangle\langle u_{d},u_{d}\rangle-\langle u_{c},u_{d}\rangle^{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p18.4" class="ltx_p">with equality if and only if <math id="S3.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=d</annotation></semantics></math>
and</p>
<table id="S3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\tr\left(\frac{1}{2}H_{f}(c,d)\right)=\langle u_{c},u_{c}\rangle+\langle u_{d},u_{d}\rangle&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\tr\left(\frac{1}{2}H_{f}(c,d)\right)=\langle u_{c},u_{c}\rangle+\langle u_{d},u_{d}\rangle&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p18.5" class="ltx_p">We conclude that the hessian is positive definite if <math id="S3.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\neq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\neq d</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, by continuity of second derivatives, the objective <math id="S3.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> must be (strongly) convex in a neighborhood of <math id="S3.p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,d)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\neq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\neq d</annotation></semantics></math>. The condition number is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(H_{f}(c,d))=\frac{1+\gamma}{1-\gamma},\quad with" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(H_{f}(c,d))=\frac{1+\gamma}{1-\gamma},\quad with</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p19" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\sqrt{1-\frac{4D}{T^{2}}}=\frac{\sqrt{\left(\langle u_{c},u_{c}\rangle-\langle u_{d},u_{d}\rangle\right)^{2}+4\langle u_{c},u_{d}\rangle^{2}}}{\langle u_{c},u_{c}\rangle+\langle u_{d},u_{d}\rangle}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\sqrt{1-\frac{4D}{T^{2}}}=\frac{\sqrt{\left(\langle u_{c},u_{c}\rangle-\langle u_{d},u_{d}\rangle\right)^{2}+4\langle u_{c},u_{d}\rangle^{2}}}{\langle u_{c},u_{c}\rangle+\langle u_{d},u_{d}\rangle}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> are continuous in <math id="S3.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,d</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that when <math id="S3.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\to d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\to d</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore <math id="S3.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(H_{f}(c,d))\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(H_{f}(c,d))\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Even though the objective can be locally convex, the condition number can still become arbitrarily large. Furthermore, this behavior can occur without poles approaching unit magnitude.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p20.1" class="ltx_p">This example is still relevant to the case with complex poles and gains, as the next lemma shows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma III.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a twice-differentiable function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{m+n}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{m+n}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> at a point <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> written in the form <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{x}(\cdot;y):\mathbb{R}^{m}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{x}(\cdot;y):\mathbb{R}^{m}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be the restriction of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to the first argument with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> fixed, i.e., <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{x}(x;y)=f(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{x}(x;y)=f(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{f}(x,y)\succeq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⪰</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{f}(x,y)\succeq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\left(H_{f}(x,y)\right)\geq\kappa\left(H_{f_{x}(\cdot;y)}(x)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\left(H_{f}(x,y)\right)\geq\kappa\left(H_{f_{x}(\cdot;y)}(x)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathbb{R}^{m+n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathbb{R}^{m+n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a vector, and consider <math id="S3.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=(u_{x},u_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=(u_{x},u_{y})</annotation></semantics></math> to be its components with <math id="S3.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda^{\text{max}}(H_{f}(x,y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda^{\text{max}}(H_{f}(x,y))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\max_{\|u\|=1}u^{\top}H_{f}(x,y)u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\max_{\|u\|=1}u^{\top}H_{f}(x,y)u</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\max_{\|u\|=1,u_{y}=0}u^{\top}H_{f}(x,y)u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\max_{\|u\|=1,u_{y}=0}u^{\top}H_{f}(x,y)u</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda^{\text{max}}\left(H_{f_{x}(\cdot;y)}(x)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda^{\text{max}}\left(H_{f_{x}(\cdot;y)}(x)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p21.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p21.2.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda^{\text{min}}(H_{f}(x,y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda^{\text{min}}(H_{f}(x,y))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\min_{\|u\|=1}u^{\top}H_{f}(x,y)u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\min_{\|u\|=1}u^{\top}H_{f}(x,y)u</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\min_{\|u\|=1,u_{y}=0}u^{\top}H_{f}(x,y)u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\min_{\|u\|=1,u_{y}=0}u^{\top}H_{f}(x,y)u</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda^{\text{min}}\left(H_{f_{x}(\cdot;y)}(x)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda^{\text{min}}\left(H_{f_{x}(\cdot;y)}(x)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p21.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p21.3.1" class="ltx_text">Combining these inequalities yields the desired result.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p22.1" class="ltx_p">The previously studied objective <math id="S3.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is a restriction of the objective <math id="S3.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> used for learning complex-valued poles and gains. Regardless of cartesian/polar pole parametrization, the condition number <math id="S3.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(H_{f}(c,d))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(H_{f}(c,d))</annotation></semantics></math> remains a lower bound on the condition number of the full Hessian since <math id="S3.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,d\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,d\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Empirical Results</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To empirically substantiate the theoretical difficulties inherent in pole learning, we consider an online real-time wireless symbol detection task. A RNN trained via BPTT is evaluated against two fixed-dynamics baselines: a randomly initialized ESN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and a domain-informed, configured ESN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, each employing 32 recurrent neurons. To analyze the training behavior of these architectures, we track the evolution of gradient norms, condition numbers, and recurrent eigenvalue spectra across training epochs. These metrics provide insight into the internal optimization dynamics of trainable RNNs and enable a direct comparison fixed pole RNNs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Left panel of Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ III Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the gradient norm <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla W_{rec}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla W_{rec}\|</annotation></semantics></math> and after early fluctuations, the gradient magnitude stabilizes at a low but non-negligible value (around <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that gradients do not vanish entirely. However, despite this, the loss remains stagnant (Right panel), suggesting that the gradients are not effective in escaping flat or ill-conditioned regions of the landscape. This observation, together with the high condition number <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(W_{\mathrm{rec}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(W_{\mathrm{rec}})</annotation></semantics></math> (around <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>) seen in the middle plot, is consistent with the presence of saddle points and poor local curvature for gradient-based optimization as predicted by the Hessian analysis in Section <a href="#S3" title="III Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>.
For the training loss plot over iterations, it is shown that there is a steep initial drop and then the loss quickly saturates, indicating that optimization is stuck in a narrow or flat region of the loss surface which again is reflecting the ill-conditioning caused by recurrent dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III Fundamental Limits of Pole Learning ‣ When Learning Hurts: Fixed-Pole RNN for Real-Time Online Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> further illustrates how trainable RNNs suffer from uncontrolled drift in pole locations. We plot the spectral density of the magnitudes of eigenvalues <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math> over various epochs. Unlike ESNs (dashed black) or configured RNNs (blue), where pole distributions are concentrated in a stable region, trainable RNNs show increasing dispersion, with eigenvalues spreading toward and beyond the unit circle. This drift reflects the absence of constraints on pole magnitudes during BPTT training and highlights the practical difficulty of maintaining stable dynamics in trainable RNNs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis demonstrates that learning internal reservoir dynamics is difficult because training objectives can be highly non-convex and ill-conditioned, posing key challenges for GD-based optimization algorithms. Furthermore, these issues can occur even without poles approaching unit magnitude. This motivates the need for fixed randomly initialized internal dynamics or domain informed configured recurrent dynamics used in ESNs. These fixed reservoir-like configurations bypass the challenges of pole learning and lead to practical and efficient real-time training.</p>
</div>
</section>
